tencentcloud:Cam/updatePasswordRuleConfi.description:
Provides a resource to manage the CAM password rules configuration (UpdatePasswordRules / GetPasswordRules).

This is a singleton, account-wide configuration resource built on the terraform-plugin-framework implementation. Create and update both call the `UpdatePasswordRules` API; read calls `GetPasswordRules`. Destroying this resource only removes it from state, the cloud-side password rules are left unchanged.

> **Note:** The CAM password rules are a single account-wide configuration. If the rules look like they have already been changed outside of Terraform, the create emits a warning and applying this configuration will overwrite them; run `terraform import tencentcloud_cam_update_password_rule_config.example cam-password-rules` and inspect the plan first if the existing values should be kept. Every attribute inside <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`rules`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Rules`" pulumi-lang-go="`rules`" pulumi-lang-python="`rules`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`rules`" pulumi-lang-java="`rules`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`rules`">`rules`</span> is optional; a value that is left unset keeps its current cloud-side setting.

> **Note:** <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`mustContain`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`MustContain`" pulumi-lang-go="`mustContain`" pulumi-lang-python="`must_contain`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`mustContain`" pulumi-lang-java="`mustContain`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`must_contain`">`mustContain`</span> is a literal character set, not an enum: write the characters the password must contain, e.g. `"Aa1!"` (upper, lower, digit, special). The console re-orders and normalizes this value (e.g. `"Aa1!"` is stored and read back as `"1!aA"`), and invalid literals are rejected by the API with `InvalidParameter.PasswordRuleError`.

